<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Tavastia</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1249 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Inland Finnish province around the lakes of Häme, brought under Swedish rule by the crusade of Birger Jarl in the mid-13th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tavastia, or Häme, covers the lake district of southern central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Finland</a>. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> its people, the Tavastians, formed one of the main Finnish groups alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> proper of the south-west and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karelians">Karelians</a> in the east. They are mentioned in Rus' chronicles as the 'Yem', against whom Novgorod campaigned from the 11th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tavastians lived from slash-and-burn farming, hunting, fishing and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, which took them far into the northern wilderness. They had no single ruler; local chiefs and kin groups led society. Papal letters of the 1230s complain of Tavastians returning to paganism and attacking Christians.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A Swedish expedition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birger-jarl">Birger Jarl</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-swedish-crusade">Second Swedish Crusade</a> and traditionally dated 1249, subdued Tavastia and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hame-castle">Häme Castle</a>. The province became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-turku">Diocese of Turku</a>, and remained so until <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Finland</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1809.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 800 CE – 1249 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"